# {{projectName}} — Spring Boot API

A Java {{#if (eq api "graphql")}}GraphQL{{else}}REST{{/if}} service generated with [JS Stack](https://createjsstack.dev).

## Requirements

- JDK 21
- Maven 3.9+

## Run it

```bash
mvn spring-boot:run
```

The API starts on `http://localhost:8080`. Health check:

```bash
curl http://localhost:8080/api/health
```

{{#if (eq api "graphql")}}
GraphQL lives at `POST /graphql`, with the GraphiQL explorer at
`http://localhost:8080/graphiql`.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq orm "jpa")}}
## Database

Spring Data JPA is configured for {{database}}. Point it at your own instance
with `DATABASE_URL`{{#if (ne database "sqlite")}}, `DATABASE_USERNAME` and `DATABASE_PASSWORD`{{/if}}.

Schema generation is set to `ddl-auto: update` for convenience. Swap it for
Flyway or Liquibase before you ship.

The sample `Todo` resource is exposed at `/api/todos`.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq database "mongodb")}}
## Database

Spring Data MongoDB connects using `MONGODB_URI`
(default `mongodb://localhost:27017/{{mavenArtifactId projectName}}`).

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq auth "spring-security")}}
## Authentication

Spring Security protects every route except `/api/health`, using stateless HTTP
Basic. No user is hard-coded — Spring Boot prints a generated password on
startup. Replace `SecurityConfig` with a real `UserDetailsService`, a JWT
decoder, or an OAuth2 resource server before production.

{{/if}}
## Tests

```bash
mvn test
```

## Build

```bash
mvn package
java -jar target/{{mavenArtifactId projectName}}-0.1.0.jar
```

## Notes

- The Java package is `com.example.app`. Rename it (and `groupId` in `pom.xml`)
  to your own namespace when you start building on it.
- CORS origins come from `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, defaulting to the usual
  frontend dev server ports.
